 trumpism but he could still  appeal to the culture war fight over  critical race theory and he won on that  basis i think a lot of party republicans  are perhaps getting a little bit excited  over excited about this and they're  saying we have a we have a way out of  rep out of trumpism in which we can sort  of take the good bits the successful  bits of trumpism electorally and we can  get rid of the nasty stuff that puts off  most voters i think there's a little bit  of over-enthusiasm there this is just  one  one result but it definitely spells  trouble for the democrats because they  are lumbered with biden who personally  invested himself in this virginia race  and who ended up  losing and the fact that his  intervention didn't just not help it  seemed to actually make the situation  worse because his popularity is so bad  so going into the midterms next year  the democrats look to be in serious  serious trouble  and jacob when you look at who voted in  virginia there's a very interesting  passion a pattern that we hadn't really  seen since trump's first election in  2016. that is that the democrat vote  turned up as strongly as they thought  but there were the republican votes  turned up in even greater numbers and  it's where it turned up that matters  we're talking about the the places  outside the cities the far stronger  rural votes in other words it seems that  the people who turned up for trump in  2016 who didn't then show up in  subsequent midterms are for some reason  coming back so the trump coalition is  coming together at the same time that  the democratic coalition is falling  apart because we're hearing lots of of  civil war in democrats if you look at  buffalo and new york you've got there  and a competition between two democrats  one a self-styled um  socialist democrat the first socialist  and leader there for like 50 years who  was taking on a centrist democrat there  for one so we're seeing civil war in the  left but cohesion on the right is